            <ArticleTitle>Protective effect of Ethyl Acetate Fraction of Drynaria quercifolia Against CCl4 induced rat Liver Fibrosis via Nrf2/ARE and NFκB signalling pathway.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0378-8741(17)32525-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jep.2017.11.015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="ETHNOPHARMACOLOGICAL RELEVANCE" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Drynaria quercifolia rhizome is traditionally used as hepatoprotective drug especially in chronic jaundice.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM OF THE STUDY" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The present study was undertaken to scientifically evaluate the efficacy of D. quercifolia rhizome against liver fibrosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">D. quercifolia rhizome crude extract (DQ) and its fractions of hexane (HDQ), ethyl acetate (EDQ), butanol (BDQ) were evaluated in vitro using primary hepatocytes and RAW 264.7 cells. Further in vivo anti-liver fibrotic activity of EDQ was assessed, monitoring serum biochemical parameters (AST, ALT, ALP, SB, cholesterol), MDA, PT, INR, GSH, SOD, CAT, liver glycogen, serum albumin levels. qRT-PCR analysis of TNF-α, COX-2, iNOS. ELISA method used to estimate TNF-α, COX-1 &amp; 2. Histopathological studies like H &amp; E, Masson's trichrome, immunohistochemistry staining for α-SMA, TIMP-1, Nrf2 was conducted. LC-QTOF-MS analysis of EDQ was conducted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In vitro activity guided fractionation of D. quercifolia revealed EDQ as active fraction when compared to other extracts. In vivo liver fibrosis studies, EDQ treatment significantly inhibit the expression of α-SMA, TIMP-1, COX-2, TNF-α, iNOS and increased the levels of Nrf2. LC-QTOF-MS analysis of EDQ confirmed the presence of naringin and naringenin.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The anti-liver fibrotic activity of EDQ via inhibition of NFκB signalling pathway, antioxidant response through Nrf2 activation and further inhibition of HSC activation.</AbstractText>
